complications arising from surgical  procedures caused by faulty devices are  rare but can happen especially as there  are some 25,000 companies trying to  place their products on the European  market in 2011 the PRP breast implant  scandal where cheap industrial silicone  was put into implants for thousands of  women to undergo surgery to have been  removed incidents such as this were  proof that legislation on medical  devices used in all types of medical  procedures ranging from surgeries of  diagnostic examinations needed an  overhaul Parliament gave its full  approval to a preliminary text in 2014  we need clear picture and we need the  possibility to give to all the patient a  clear view of what we are going to  implant in their body in terms of  medical device in the past we have no  security not a process of authorization  of the devices obligations will be  placed on manufacturers to ensure safety  as well as random inspections on devices  patients and doctors will also be given  information on implants used to increase  traceability last autumn final  negotiations between Parliament and  member states began the two institutions  agreed to place a legal obligation on  the EU Commission to draw up a list of  devices that cannot be reused but they  use executive has some reservations they  want a negative list where one not to  have the possibility to repossess some  devices it is estimated that by 2060  there will be double the number of  people aged over 65 years old and so  ensuring medical devices are of the best  quality is of utmost importance
 

